MoLow third-party notices
=========================

MoLow itself is licensed under the BSD 3-Clause License. See LICENSE.

This file lists the third-party components that are compiled into or shipped
with a MoLow build, the license each one carries, and what that license asks of
anyone redistributing a build. Version numbers match go.mod and
frontend/package.json at the time of writing; run `go list -m all` and read
frontend/bun.lock for the exact resolved set.

The MIT and BSD-3-Clause components only require that their copyright notice and
license text travel with the source, and that the license text travels with a
binary. Every one of them is vendored under the module cache and mirrored in the
Go module proxy, so the notice obligation is met by not stripping the licenses
from the source tree.

The two sections below are the ones that need action beyond that.


Copyleft components
-------------------

### github.com/tphakala/go-aac v0.7.0 — LGPL-2.1-or-later

Reached through the engine's `decode` package, so it is linked into every build
that can play AAC, M4A or MP4.

go-aac is a derivative work of FFmpeg's LGPL-licensed AAC codec and cannot be
relicensed permissively. It is used unmodified, as a library, with no changes to
its source.

If you redistribute a MoLow binary, you must:

1. Ship the LGPL-2.1 text. It is at
   https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/lgpl-2.1.txt and is also included in
   the go-aac distribution.
2. State that go-aac is used, under LGPL-2.1-or-later, and name the version.
3. Make the corresponding source available. Pointing at the upstream module at
   the pinned version satisfies this:
   https://github.com/tphakala/go-aac/tree/v0.7.0
4. Not impose terms that forbid reverse engineering for the purpose of debugging
   modifications to the LGPL component. The BSD 3-Clause License places no such
   restriction, so a stock MoLow build already complies.

Go links statically, so there is no separate shared object to swap. If you need
to replace go-aac with your own build, rebuild MoLow with a `replace` directive
pointing at your copy; no source change is required.

### github.com/dlcuy22/ytm-go v1.3.0 — MIT

Listed here only because earlier versions (up to and including v1.2.1) were
GPL-3.0. v1.3.0 relicensed the library to MIT, which is what MoLow requires, so
the whole distributed work can carry BSD 3-Clause. Do not downgrade below
v1.3.0: a GPL-3.0 dependency would make the combined binary GPL-3.0.


Apache-2.0 component
--------------------

### github.com/ebitengine/oto/v3 v3.5.0 — Apache-2.0

Reached through the engine's audio backend. Apache-2.0 is compatible with BSD
3-Clause. Redistributing a binary requires shipping the Apache-2.0 license text
and preserving any NOTICE file oto ships.

oto is also what carries the platform audio bindings: WASAPI on Windows,
PulseAudio on Linux, CoreAudio on macOS.


